The Agent Control Plane: A Shield-Aware Constrained MDP Framework for Governed Autonomous Execution

Ouadi Maakoul

Version 3.1 

AI : chatGpt, Grok, Gemini, Meta AI , Deepseek 

Abstract

Autonomous AI agents execute consequential actions under delegated human authority. The central problem is not agent capability but agent authority: how to guarantee that an agent's actions remain within the bounds of what it has been authorized to do, regardless of what it has learned or been prompted to attempt. This monograph develops the Agent Control Plane (ACP), a formally grounded control layer between agent intention and real-world execution. We make six foundational contributions. First, we introduce the shield-aware constrained MDP (SA-CMDP) with a shield-learnability condition that replaces the value-dominance assumption, ensuring convergence under both tabular and neural policy parameterizations. Second, we formalize distributional shielding for differentiable policy classes, restoring the smoothness conditions required for natural policy gradient convergence. Third, we prove that delegated authority forms a complete product lattice over eight constraint dimensions, with a canonical serialization enabling cryptographically verifiable authorization witnesses. Fourth, we develop a regularized anytime-valid e-process that incorporates abstraction-derived prior knowledge without sacrificing anytime-validity, extending monitoring from marginal to state-dependent safety verification. Fifth, we specify a bifurcated execution architecture with compensability classification, saga-style compensation for reversible actions, and human approval routing for irreversible actions. Sixth, we provide a hardened kernel-level enforcement specification addressing known seccomp-bpf bypass vectors. The thesis defended is that agent intelligence and agent authority are distinct computational objects, and that governing the transition from reasoning to execution requires a control plane with its own formal foundations.

