1. Introduction

1.1 The Authority Problem

The deployment of LLM-based agents capable of executing code, making purchases, sending messages, and coordinating with other agents creates authorization problems that existing access control frameworks were not designed to handle. Unlike traditional software principals, LLM agents are non-deterministic, prompt-sensitive, and capable of generating novel action sequences. The principle of least privilege establishes that delegated capabilities should be attenuable but not amplifiable.

The Agent Control Plane (ACP) treats authority as a first-class computational object. It is an admission-controlled intermediary that decides, before a tool invocation executes, whether the invocation is cryptographically authorized, procedurally compliant, instantaneously safe, and within acceptable predicted risk.

1.2 Design Axioms

A1. Safety is enforced, not optimized. Optimization shapes policy; the shield enforces it.

A2. Identity, capability, and execution evidence are cryptographically distinct.

A3. Every consequential decision is trace-bound, hash-chained, and replay-verifiable.

A4. Latency budgets are explicit and separated by pipeline stage.

A5. Formal goals are authored, not auto-generated, for high-risk envelopes.

A6. The control plane is on the network path, not beside it.

1.3 Central Invariant

\text{Execute}(q) \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{ValidIdentity} \wedge \text{ValidCapability} \wedge \text{ValidDelegationChain} \wedge \text{Feasible}(q) \wedge \text{AcceptableRisk}

1.4 Central Principle

\text{Agent Intelligence} \neq \text{Agent Authority}

Intelligence proposes. Policy constrains. Humans retain authority over consequential decisions.

1.5 Contributions

1. Shield-aware CMDP with learnability condition: A composite decision process in which the shield is integrated into the transition kernel and into the learning signal, ensuring convergence without relying on value-dominance assumptions.
2. Distributional shielding for neural policies: A differentiable shield operator acting on policy distributions, restoring smoothness conditions for natural policy gradient convergence.
3. Complete eight-dimensional capability lattice: A product lattice formalization with canonical serialization, monotone attenuation proofs, and machine-readable denial diagnostics.
4. Regularized anytime-valid sequential monitoring: A knowledge-augmented e-process that extends marginal safety-rate verification to state-dependent monitoring while preserving anytime-validity.
5. Compensability-aware bifurcated execution: A two-path admission pipeline with compensability classification, saga-style compensation for reversible actions, and mandatory human approval for irreversible actions.
6. Hardened kernel-level non-bypassability: A seccomp-bpf specification addressing x32 ABI aliasing, file descriptor remapping, and foreign-ABI bypass vectors.

