3. Capability Lattice for Delegation

3.1 Eight-Dimensional Faceted Authority

Delegation chains must enforce that delegated capabilities can only be attenuated, never amplified. We formalize agent authority over eight constraint dimensions as a product lattice.

Definition 3.1 (Faceted Capability). A capability is a tuple:

C = (R, A, \mathcal{C}_{\text{spend}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{depth}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{time}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{rep}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{val}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{rev}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{comp}})

where:

· R \in \mathcal{R} is a resource identifier in the prefix-tree lattice (nodes are resource paths, containment is ancestor-descendant).
· A \subseteq \mathcal{A} is a set of actions with subset inclusion order.
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{spend}} \in [0, \infty] with reverse order.
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{depth}} \in \mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\} with reverse order.
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{time}} \subseteq \mathbb{T} is a set of allowed time intervals.
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{rep}} \in [0,1] with direct order.
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{val}} \subseteq \mathcal{V} is a set of value constraints.
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{rev}} \in [0,1] with direct order.
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{comp}} \in \{\text{full}, \text{partial}, \text{none}\} is the compensability classification.

Definition 3.2 (Faceted Capability Order). Define the partial order \preceq on capabilities:

C_1 \preceq C_2 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\begin{cases}
R_1 \subseteq_{\text{prefix}} R_2, \\
A_1 \subseteq A_2, \\
\mathcal{C}_{1,\text{spend}} \le \mathcal{C}_{2,\text{spend}}, \\
\mathcal{C}_{1,\text{depth}} \le \mathcal{C}_{2,\text{depth}}, \\
\mathcal{C}_{1,\text{time}} \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{2,\text{time}}, \\
\mathcal{C}_{1,\text{rep}} \ge \mathcal{C}_{2,\text{rep}}, \\
\mathcal{C}_{1,\text{val}} \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{2,\text{val}}, \\
\mathcal{C}_{1,\text{rev}} \ge \mathcal{C}_{2,\text{rev}}, \\
\mathcal{C}_{1,\text{comp}} \preceq_{\text{comp}} \mathcal{C}_{2,\text{comp}}
\end{cases}

where \preceq_{\text{comp}} orders compensability from \text{none} \preceq \text{partial} \preceq \text{full}.

Theorem 3.1 (Faceted Capability Lattice). The set of capabilities (\mathcal{C}, \preceq) forms a complete lattice.

Proof. Each component has a complete lattice structure. The resource order (\mathcal{R}, \subseteq) is a complete lattice with join R_1 \vee R_2 = R_1 \cup R_2 and meet R_1 \wedge R_2 = R_1 \cap R_2. The action order (2^{\mathcal{A}}, \subseteq) is a powerset lattice. The spend order ([0, \infty], \le) with reverse order is a complete lattice with meet \max and join \min. The depth order (\mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}, \le) with reverse order is a complete lattice. The time order (2^{\mathbb{T}}, \subseteq) is a powerset lattice. The reputation order ([0,1], \ge) is a complete lattice with meet \min and join \max. The values order (2^{\mathcal{V}}, \subseteq) is a powerset lattice. The reversibility order ([0,1], \ge) is a complete lattice. The compensability order \{\text{none}, \text{partial}, \text{full}\} with \preceq_{\text{comp}} is a complete lattice. The product of complete lattices is a complete lattice. \square

Definition 3.3 (Delegation as Monotone Function). A delegation from parent capability C_p to child capability C_c is valid iff C_c \preceq C_p. Delegation is a monotone narrowing function on the capability lattice.

Theorem 3.2 (Privilege Non-Escalation). Let each delegation edge (T_i \to T_{i+1}) be signed with an EUF-CMA-secure signature scheme. Let capability attenuation be monotone: for every parent/child pair, C_{i+1} \preceq C_i. Then communication between agents cannot grant new privileges absent an explicit signed delegation.

Proof. Suppose agent A_1 sends a message m to A_2, and A_2 attempts to execute an action a with capability C_a \not\preceq C_1. By the central invariant, execution requires a valid delegation chain C' = (C_0 \to C_1 \to \cdots \to C_n) where C_n \succeq C_a and every edge is signed. The chain must originate from a trusted root. By monotone attenuation, C_n \preceq C_0. The message m is not a signed delegation edge; it does not appear in any chain. Therefore, the chain C' must contain a signed edge (T_1 \to T_2) where T_1 is an ancestor of A_1 and T_2 is A_2. If such an edge does not exist, A_2 must forge it. Forging the signature reduces to the EUF-CMA security of the signature scheme, which succeeds with negligible probability. \square

Corollary 3.1 (Communication ≠ Authorization). Message passing does not confer authority. Authority flows only through signed delegation edges.

3.2 Canonical Serialization and Authorization Witness

Definition 3.4 (Canonical Capability Serialization). The canonical serialization of a capability C = (R, A, \mathcal{C}_{\text{spend}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{depth}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{time}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{rep}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{val}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{rev}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{comp}}) is a byte string \text{ser}(C) constructed as follows:

1. Dimensions are sorted lexicographically by their names.
2. Resource identifiers are serialized as UTF-8 strings with length prefixes.
3. Action sets are sorted lexicographically, deduplicated, and serialized as length-prefixed concatenations.
4. Numeric values are encoded as 64-bit big-endian integers (spend in micro-units, depth as unsigned, reputation and reversibility as 16-bit fixed-point).
5. Time intervals are sorted by start time, deduplicated, and serialized as 64-bit timestamps.
6. Value constraints are sorted lexicographically and serialized as length-prefixed strings.
7. Compensability is serialized as a single byte: 0x00 for none, 0x01 for partial, 0x02 for full.

The lattice position is the SHA-256 hash of \text{ser}(C).

Theorem 3.3 (Serialization Determinism). For any two capabilities C_1, C_2, if C_1 = C_2 as tuples, then \text{ser}(C_1) = \text{ser}(C_2) as byte strings.

Proof. The serialization algorithm is deterministic: it sorts all set-valued dimensions, uses fixed-width numeric encodings, and applies a canonical ordering to all components. Therefore, identical capabilities produce identical serializations. \square

Definition 3.5 (Authorization Witness). An authorization witness is a tuple:

W = \langle \text{lattice\_position}, \text{constraint\_vector\_hash}, \text{parent\_witness\_hash} \rangle

where lattice_position is the SHA-256 hash of the canonical capability serialization, constraint_vector_hash is the SHA-256 hash of the eight-dimensional constraint vector, and parent_witness_hash is the hash of the parent's authorization witness. The witness enables structured denial diagnostics and near-miss detection.

3.3 Revocation

Each IBCT carries a chain ID (CID). A Bloom filter provides sub-millisecond membership queries at the fast path. For applications requiring certainty, a signed CRL with a monotone epoch counter is appended and Merkle-rooted.

Theorem 3.4 (Revocation Soundness). Given a Bloom filter \mathcal{B} of revoked chain IDs, no revoked chain is executed:

\text{CID}(C) \in \mathcal{B} \;\Rightarrow\; \text{Verify}(C) = \text{DENY}.

Proof. Bloom filters have no false negatives for inserted elements. If \text{CID}(C) is revoked, it is inserted into \mathcal{B}, and \mathcal{B}.\text{contains}(\text{CID}(C)) returns true. The verification procedure checks \mathcal{B}.\text{contains}(\text{CID}(C)) and returns DENY if true. \square

Remark 3.1 (False Positives). Bloom filters have a bounded false-positive rate \varepsilon_{\text{fp}}. A non-revoked chain may be incorrectly denied with probability \varepsilon_{\text{fp}}. This is a liveness cost, not a safety cost.

