5. Technical Specifications

5.1 Hardened Kernel-Level Enforcement

The ACP gateway enforces non-bypassability at the kernel level using seccomp-bpf filters and mandatory access control. However, seccomp-bpf is not a panacea. Known bypass vectors include:

· x32 ABI syscall number aliasing: Most BPF filters only check the low 32 bits against known syscall numbers, missing the x32 variants. This allows an attacker to invoke a blocked syscall via its x32 alias.
· 32-bit argument check bypass: BPF filter code generators may only check the lower 32 bits of syscall arguments on x86_64, allowing filter bypass.
· File descriptor remapping: An attacker can use fcntl or dup2 to remap a restricted file descriptor to an unrestricted one, circumventing path-based restrictions.

Hardening Specification 5.1. The seccomp filter must:

1. Validate the ABI: Check AUDIT_ARCH and mask off the x32 bit before comparing syscall numbers. Return ENOSYS for any syscall not originating from the native x86_64 ABI.
2. Check full 64-bit arguments: Use BPF load instructions that read both the low and high 32 bits of syscall arguments, rather than only the low 32 bits.
3. Restrict FD manipulation: Use SECCOMP_RET_TRACE for dup, dup2, dup3, and fcntl to prevent file descriptor remapping. Combined with Landlock for filesystem confinement.
4. Enforce network allowlist: Use a cgroup BPF egress firewall at the connect4/connect6 hooks with a per-session network allowlist. Unlisted destinations receive EPERM before the socket is established.

Formal Non-Bypassability. The non-bypassability property is:

\text{Execute}(q) \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{seccomp}_{\text{hardened}}(q) \,\wedge\, \text{landlock}(q) \,\wedge\, \text{netfilter}(q) \,\wedge\, \text{ACP-admitted}(q).

The agent's syscall surface is restricted to \Sigma_{\text{allowed}} \subseteq \Sigma_{\text{all}}. Any q requiring \sigma \notin \Sigma_{\text{allowed}} is blocked at the kernel boundary with EPERM, before the ACP is consulted.

5.2 Goal Envelope AST: Formal Semantics

Raw NL→LTL achieves 24–35% semantic correctness. We forbid this path for authoritative goals. Goals are declared as parameterized templates that compile deterministically to LTL_f.

Definition 5.1 (Goal Envelope Denotation). Each template T with parameters p denotes a set of finite traces \llbracket T(p) \rrbracket \subseteq (2^{\mathrm{AP}})^*. The denotation is defined recursively:

· \llbracket \text{BOUNDED\_ACTION\_SEQUENCE}(A, M, F) \rrbracket = \{\tau \mid \forall t: \tau_t \in A,\ \forall a: \#_a(\tau) \le M[a],\ \forall (f,t) \in F: \neg(f \prec t \text{ in } \tau)\}.
· \llbracket \text{DENY\_TRANSITION}(f, t) \rrbracket = \{\tau \mid \forall t': \tau_{t'} = f \Rightarrow \tau_{t'+1} \neq t\}.
· \llbracket \text{REQUIRE\_PRIOR}(p, t) \rrbracket = \{\tau \mid \forall t': \tau_{t'} = t \Rightarrow \exists t'' < t': \tau_{t''} = p\}.
· \llbracket \text{RATE\_LIMIT}(a, m, w) \rrbracket = \{\tau \mid \forall t': \#_a(\tau_{t' \ldots t'+w}) \le m\}.

Definition 5.2 (Compilation Function). The compilation function \text{compile}: \text{Template} \to \text{LTL}_f is defined by the schemas in Appendix H. The compilation is sound if for every template T and parameters p:

\tau \models \text{compile}(T(p)) \;\Longrightarrow\; \tau \in \llbracket T(p) \rrbracket

and complete if:

\tau \in \llbracket T(p) \rrbracket \;\Longrightarrow\; \tau \models \text{compile}(T(p)).

Theorem 5.1 (Soundness and Completeness of Compilation). The compilation function is sound and complete for all templates in the Goal Envelope AST.

Proof. By structural induction on the template grammar. For each template, the LTL_f schema is constructed to match the denotation exactly. The base cases (BOUNDED_ACTION_SEQUENCE, DENY_TRANSITION, etc.) follow directly from the definitions. The compositional cases (AND, OR, UNTIL) preserve soundness and completeness by the induction hypothesis and the semantics of LTL_f connectives. \square

5.3 LTL_f Monitoring: Two-Tier Architecture

We specify a two-tier monitoring architecture:

· Fast path (automaton-based): For templates compiled from the Goal Envelope AST, the automata are small and known in advance. The monitor maintains the automaton state q_t incrementally: q_{t+1} = \delta(q_t, L(s_t, a_t)). This is \mathcal{O}(1) per step with a hash-table representation of \delta.
· Slow path (trace-checking): For full-trace evaluation, we use the automata-less trace-checking approach. For LTL_f, all safety and cosafety formulas are intentionally safe and cosafe, so monitoring can be carried out directly on the trace with polynomial complexity in the size of the trace and the formula, without constructing a DFA.

Theorem 5.2 (Trace-Checking Complexity for LTL_f). For any LTL_f safety or cosafety formula \varphi, monitoring can be carried out by direct trace-checking with polynomial complexity in |\varphi| and |\tau|, without constructing a DFA.

Proof. The trace-checking problem reduces to evaluating the formula on the finite trace using dynamic programming over the formula structure. The dynamic program has state space O(|\varphi| \cdot |\tau|) subproblems, with O(1) transition per subproblem. Total complexity is O(|\varphi| \cdot |\tau|), which is polynomial in both the formula size and the trace length. \square

