6. Conclusion

The Agent Control Plane provides a formally grounded control layer for autonomous AI agents. Its six theoretical pillars—shield-aware CMDP with learnability conditions, distributional shielding for differentiable policies, component-wise PAC abstraction bounds, regularized anytime-valid e-process monitoring, compensability-aware bifurcated execution, and hardened kernel-level non-bypassability—address the fundamental soundness gaps that arise when optimization and enforcement are conflated, when state abstractions are assumed rather than verified, when delegation is checked ad hoc, and when runtime monitoring is confused with offline certification.

The central thesis is that agent intelligence and agent authority are distinct computational objects. Intelligence proposes; policy constrains; humans retain authority over consequential decisions. Governing the transition from reasoning to execution requires a control plane with its own formal foundations, not an extension of the agent's reasoning capabilities.

\text{Agent Intelligence} \neq \text{Agent Authority}

