Appendix A: Proofs for Section 2 (Mathematical Foundations)

A.1 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Well-Definedness of SA-CMDP)

Proposition 2.1. The shield-aware CMDP \mathcal{M}_c^\sigma is well-defined: P^\sigma is a valid transition kernel, and r^\sigma, c_k^\sigma are bounded measurable functions.

Proof. We verify each component.

(i) P^\sigma is a valid transition kernel. For each (s,a) \in S \times \mathcal{A}, the shield produces \sigma(s,a) \in \mathcal{A} \cup \{\bot\}. We define P^\sigma as:

P^\sigma(s' \mid s, a) = \begin{cases} P(s' \mid s, \sigma(s,a)), & \sigma(s,a) \in \mathcal{A}, \\[4pt] \mathbb{1}[s' = s_\bot], & \sigma(s,a) = \bot, \end{cases}

where s_\bot \in S is a designated absorbing failure state, assumed to exist in S. For each (s,a):

· Non-negativity: P(s' \mid s, \sigma(s,a)) \ge 0 for all s', and \mathbb{1}[s' = s_\bot] \ge 0.
· Normalization: If \sigma(s,a) \in \mathcal{A}, then \sum_{s'} P(s' \mid s, \sigma(s,a)) = 1 since P is a valid kernel. If \sigma(s,a) = \bot, then \sum_{s'} \mathbb{1}[s' = s_\bot] = 1.
· Measurability: \sigma: S \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A} \cup \{\bot\} is assumed measurable. The composition s' \mapsto P(s' \mid s, \sigma(s,a)) is measurable in (s,a) because P is a transition kernel and \sigma is measurable. The indicator \mathbb{1}[s' = s_\bot] is constant in (s,a) and hence measurable.

Therefore P^\sigma is a valid transition kernel on S \times \mathcal{A}.

(ii) r^\sigma is bounded and measurable. r^\sigma(s,a) = r(s, \sigma(s,a)) - \kappa \cdot \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s,a) \neq a]. Since r is bounded (there exists R_{\max} < \infty with |r(s,a)| \le R_{\max} for all (s,a)) and \kappa > 0 is a constant, we have |r^\sigma(s,a)| \le R_{\max} + \kappa. Measurability follows from the composition of the measurable function \sigma with the measurable function r, and the indicator \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s,a) \neq a] is measurable.

(iii) c_k^\sigma are bounded and measurable. Identical to (ii) with c_k in place of r. Since c_k \ge 0 and bounded (there exists C_{k,\max} < \infty with 0 \le c_k(s,a) \le C_{k,\max}), c_k^\sigma inherits these properties. \square

A.2 Proof of Lemma 2.1 (Sufficient Condition for Shield Learnability)

Lemma 2.1. Suppose the shield satisfies: for all (s,a) \in S \times \mathcal{A} and all k,

c_k(s, \sigma(s,a)) \le c_k(s,a)
\quad\text{and}\quad
\mathbb{E}_{s' \sim P(\cdot \mid s, \sigma(s,a))}\!\left[V_{c_k}^{\sigma,\pi}(s')\right] \le \mathbb{E}_{s' \sim P(\cdot \mid s, a)}\!\left[V_{c_k}^{\sigma,\pi}(s')\right] + \frac{\kappa}{1-\gamma}.

Then V_{c_k}^{\sigma,\pi}(s) \le V_{c_k}^{\pi}(s) + \frac{\kappa}{1-\gamma} \cdot \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s_t,a_t) \neq a_t]\right] for all s.

Proof. By induction on the Bellman operator. Define the operator \mathcal{T}^\sigma acting on bounded measurable functions V: S \to \mathbb{R} by:

(\mathcal{T}^\sigma V)(s) = \mathbb{E}_{a \sim \pi}\!\left[c_k(s, \sigma(s,a)) + \kappa \cdot \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s,a) \neq a] + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{s' \sim P(\cdot \mid s, \sigma(s,a))}\!\left[V(s')\right]\right].

The shielded cost value function V_{c_k}^{\sigma,\pi} is the unique fixed point of \mathcal{T}^\sigma (by the Banach fixed-point theorem, since \mathcal{T}^\sigma is a \gamma-contraction in the sup-norm). Similarly, define:

(\mathcal{T} V)(s) = \mathbb{E}_{a \sim \pi}\!\left[c_k(s, a) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{s' \sim P(\cdot \mid s, a)}\!\left[V(s')\right]\right].

The unshielded cost value function V_{c_k}^{\pi} is the unique fixed point of \mathcal{T}.

Assume for induction that V_{c_k}^{\sigma,\pi}(s') \le V_{c_k}^{\pi}(s') + \frac{\kappa}{1-\gamma} \cdot I(s') for all s', where I(s') = \mathbb{E}_\pi[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s_t,a_t) \neq a_t] \mid s_0 = s'] is the discounted intervention frequency from s'.

Then:

\begin{aligned}
V_{c_k}^{\sigma,\pi}(s) &= \mathbb{E}_{a \sim \pi}\!\left[c_k(s, \sigma(s,a)) + \kappa \cdot \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s,a) \neq a] + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{s'}\!\left[V_{c_k}^{\sigma,\pi}(s')\right]\right] \\
&\le \mathbb{E}_{a \sim \pi}\!\left[c_k(s, a) + \kappa \cdot \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s,a) \neq a] + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{s'}\!\left[V_{c_k}^{\pi}(s') + \frac{\kappa}{1-\gamma} I(s')\right]\right] \\
&= \mathbb{E}_{a \sim \pi}\!\left[c_k(s, a) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{s'}\!\left[V_{c_k}^{\pi}(s')\right]\right] + \kappa \cdot \mathbb{E}_{a \sim \pi}\!\left[\mathbb{1}[\sigma(s,a) \neq a]\right] + \frac{\gamma \kappa}{1-\gamma} \mathbb{E}_{a \sim \pi}\!\left[\mathbb{E}_{s'}\!\left[I(s')\right]\right] \\
&= V_{c_k}^{\pi}(s) + \kappa \cdot \mathbb{E}_\pi[\mathbb{1}[\sigma(s,a) \neq a]] + \frac{\gamma \kappa}{1-\gamma} \mathbb{E}_\pi[I(s')] \\
&= V_{c_k}^{\pi}(s) + \frac{\kappa}{1-\gamma} \cdot \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s_t,a_t) \neq a_t]\right],
\end{aligned}

where the last equality uses the recursive structure I(s) = \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s,a) \neq a] + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{s'}[I(s')]. The base case follows from the boundedness of c_k and the contraction property of the Bellman operator. \square

A.3 Proof of Theorem 2.1 (Slater Preservation under Learnability)

Theorem 2.1. Let the shield be defined by cost-threshold infeasibility:

\mathcal{A}_{\text{feas}}(s) = \{a \in \mathcal{A} \mid \forall k: c_k(s,a) \le d_k^{\text{inst}}\}.

If the unshielded CMDP satisfies Slater's condition (there exists \pi with J_{c_k}(\pi) < d_k for all k) and Assumption 2.1 holds, then \mathcal{M}_c^\sigma satisfies Slater's condition provided that:

\kappa \cdot \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s_t,a_t) \neq a_t]\right] < \min_k (d_k - J_{c_k}(\pi)).

Proof. Let \pi be a strictly feasible policy of \mathcal{M}_c with J_{c_k}(\pi) < d_k for all k. Under Assumption 2.1:

J_{c_k}^\sigma(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{s_0}\!\left[V_{c_k}^{\sigma,\pi}(s_0)\right] \le \mathbb{E}_{s_0}\!\left[V_{c_k}^{\pi}(s_0)\right] + \kappa \cdot \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s_t,a_t) \neq a_t]\right] = J_{c_k}(\pi) + \Delta_k,

where \Delta_k = \kappa \cdot \mathbb{E}_\pi[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \mathbb{1}[\sigma(s_t,a_t) \neq a_t]]. If \Delta_k < d_k - J_{c_k}(\pi) for all k, then J_{c_k}^\sigma(\pi) < d_k for all k. Therefore \pi is strictly feasible in \mathcal{M}_c^\sigma, and Slater's condition is preserved. \square

A.4 Proof of Theorem 2.2 (Differentiability Preservation)

Theorem 2.2. If the distributional shield \sigma_{\text{dist}} is Lipschitz continuous with respect to the policy parameters w, then the shielded policy class \{\sigma_{\text{dist}} \circ \pi_w : w \in \Theta\} inherits the smoothness properties of the unshielded policy class.

Proof. Let \pi_w be parameterized by w. The shielded policy is \pi_w^\sigma = \sigma_{\text{dist}} \circ \pi_w. For any w, w' \in \Theta:

\|\pi_w^\sigma - \pi_{w'}^\sigma\|_{\mathrm{TV}} = \|\sigma_{\text{dist}}(\pi_w) - \sigma_{\text{dist}}(\pi_{w'})\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le L_\sigma \|\pi_w - \pi_{w'}\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le L_\sigma L_\pi \|w - w'\|,

where L_\sigma is the Lipschitz constant of \sigma_{\text{dist}} with respect to the total variation norm, and L_\pi is the smoothness constant of the unshielded policy class. Therefore the shielded class satisfies the same Lipschitz continuity with constant L_\sigma L_\pi. \square

A.5 Proof of Theorem 2.3 (Shield-Aware Convergence with Distributional Shield)

Theorem 2.3. Under Slater's condition, smooth policy parameterization (preserved by Theorem 2.2), bounded rewards and costs, compact action set, and Lipschitz continuity of the Lagrangian, the shield-aware CMDP \mathcal{M}_c^\sigma admits global convergence of NPG-PD with sublinear rates:

J_r^{\sigma,*} - J_r^\sigma(\pi_T) \le \frac{C_1}{\sqrt{T}}, \qquad \big[J_{c_k}^\sigma(\pi_T) - d_k\big]^+ \le \frac{C_2}{\sqrt{T}}.

Proof. We verify that the conditions (C1)–(C5) of the NPG-PD convergence theorem are preserved under the distributional shield.

(C1) Slater's condition. Established by Theorem 2.1 under the intervention-frequency bound.

(C2) Smooth policy parameterization. Preserved by Theorem 2.2. The distributional shield is Lipschitz continuous with respect to the policy parameters, so the shielded policy class inherits smoothness.

(C3) Bounded rewards and costs. r^\sigma and c_k^\sigma are bounded by the same constants as r and c_k, plus the intervention penalty \kappa (Proposition 2.1).

(C4) Compact action set. The effective action set is \mathcal{A}_{\text{feas}}(s) \subseteq \mathcal{A}, which is compact.

(C5) Lipschitz continuity of the Lagrangian. The Lagrangian:

\mathcal{L}^\sigma(\pi, \lambda) = J_r^\sigma(\pi) - \sum_k \lambda_k (J_{c_k}^\sigma(\pi) - d_k)

inherits Lipschitz continuity from \mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda) because the distributional shield is Lipschitz continuous with respect to the policy parameters. Formally, for any w, w':

|\mathcal{L}^\sigma(\pi_w, \lambda) - \mathcal{L}^\sigma(\pi_{w'}, \lambda)| \le L_\sigma \|\pi_w - \pi_{w'}\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le L_\sigma L_\pi \|w - w'\|.

Under (C1)–(C5), the NPG-PD convergence theorem of Ding et al. (2025, Theorem 4.3) applies to \mathcal{M}_c^\sigma, yielding the stated rates with constants C_1, C_2 depending on L_\sigma, L_\pi, R_{\max}, C_{k,\max}, \gamma, and d_k, but independent of the state-action space size. \square

A.6 Proof of Theorem 2.4 (Component-Wise PAC Bound)

We first state the empirical Bernstein inequality.

Lemma A.1 (Empirical Bernstein Inequality). Let X_1, \ldots, X_n be i.i.d. random variables with values in [0,1], mean \mu, and observed variance:

\hat{\sigma}^2 = \frac{1}{n-1} \sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \bar{X})^2, \qquad \bar{X} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n X_i.

Then with probability at least 1 - \delta:

|\bar{X} - \mu| \le \sqrt{\frac{2\hat{\sigma}^2 \log(2/\delta)}{n}} + \frac{7 \log(2/\delta)}{3(n-1)}.

Proof. See Maurer and Pontil (2009, Theorem 4). \square

Lemma A.2 (Lipschitz Continuity of Satisfaction Probability). For a finite-horizon DTMC with horizon H, the satisfaction probability P_{\mathcal{M}}[\psi] is Lipschitz continuous with respect to the transition matrix P:

|P_{\hat{\mathcal{M}}}[\psi] - P_{\mathcal{M}}[\psi]| \le \sum_{i,j} L_\psi^{(ij)} H |\hat{P}(j \mid i) - P(j \mid i)|,

where L_\psi^{(ij)} is the sensitivity of the satisfaction probability to transition (i,j).

Proof. The satisfaction probability can be written as:

P_{\mathcal{M}}[\psi] = \sum_{\tau \in \text{Traces}(H)} \Pr\nolimits_{\mathcal{M}}[\tau] \cdot \mathbb{1}[\tau \models \psi],

where the sum is over all traces of length H. The probability of a trace \tau = (s_0, s_1, \ldots, s_H) under \mathcal{M} is:

\Pr\nolimits_{\mathcal{M}}[\tau] = \mu(s_0) \prod_{t=0}^{H-1} P(s_{t+1} \mid s_t).

Perturbing P to \hat{P} changes each factor by at most |\hat{P}(j \mid i) - P(j \mid i)|. By the product rule and the triangle inequality, the change in the trace probability is bounded by:

|\Pr\nolimits_{\hat{\mathcal{M}}}[\tau] - \Pr\nolimits_{\mathcal{M}}[\tau]| \le \sum_{t=0}^{H-1} \prod_{t' \neq t} P(s_{t'+1} \mid s_{t'}) \cdot |\hat{P}(s_{t+1} \mid s_t) - P(s_{t+1} \mid s_t)|.

Summing over traces and using the sensitivity weights L_\psi^{(ij)} yields the result. \square

Theorem 2.4 (Component-Wise PAC Bound). For a DTMC over N abstract states learned from n traces per state-action pair, with observed per-transition variance \hat{\sigma}_{ij}^2, with probability \ge 1-\delta:

\left|P_{\hat{\mathcal{M}}}[\psi] - P_{\mathcal{M}}[\psi]\right| \le \sum_{i,j} L_\psi^{(ij)} H \left( \sqrt{\frac{2\hat{\sigma}_{ij}^2 \log(2/\delta_{ij})}{n_{ij}}} + \frac{7\log(2/\delta_{ij})}{3(n_{ij}-1)} \right) + L_\psi \varepsilon_{\text{abs}}

where \delta_{ij} is the per-entry error budget satisfying \sum_{i,j} \delta_{ij} \le \delta, and L_\psi^{(ij)} is the sensitivity of the satisfaction probability to transition (i,j).

Proof. Apply Lemma A.1 to each transition entry (i,j) with error budget \delta_{ij}:

|\hat{P}(j \mid i) - P(j \mid i)| \le \sqrt{\frac{2\hat{\sigma}_{ij}^2 \log(2/\delta_{ij})}{n_{ij}}} + \frac{7 \log(2/\delta_{ij})}{3(n_{ij}-1)}

with probability at least 1 - \delta_{ij}. By the union bound, all N^2 entries satisfy their respective bounds with probability at least:

1 - \sum_{i,j} \delta_{ij} \ge 1 - \delta.

Combining with Lemma A.2 (Lipschitz propagation) and adding the abstraction error L_\psi \varepsilon_{\text{abs}} yields the result. \square

A.7 Proof of Corollary 2.4 (Sample Complexity Reduction)

Corollary 2.4. The component-wise bound requires n_{ij} \ge \frac{7\log(2/\delta_{ij})}{3} \left(\frac{L_\psi^{(ij)} H}{\varepsilon_{ij} - L_\psi \varepsilon_{\text{abs}}}\right) samples for transition (i,j).

Proof. Set the per-entry error bound equal to \varepsilon_{ij}:

L_\psi^{(ij)} H \left( \sqrt{\frac{2\hat{\sigma}_{ij}^2 \log(2/\delta_{ij})}{n_{ij}}} + \frac{7\log(2/\delta_{ij})}{3(n_{ij}-1)} \right) \le \varepsilon_{ij}.

For near-deterministic transitions (\hat{\sigma}_{ij}^2 \approx 0), the second term dominates, yielding:

n_{ij} \ge \frac{7 L_\psi^{(ij)} H \log(2/\delta_{ij})}{3 \varepsilon_{ij}}.

Allocating the error budget \varepsilon_{ij} \propto L_\psi^{(ij)} and using \sum_{i,j} \varepsilon_{ij} = \varepsilon - L_\psi \varepsilon_{\text{abs}} yields the stated bound. \square

A.8 Proof of Theorem 2.5 (E-Process Validity)

Theorem 2.5. Under H_0: P_{\text{safe}} \ge \theta, the e-process E_t = \prod_{i=0}^{t} E_i with E_i = 1 + \beta_i (X_i - \theta) is a non-negative supermartingale with \mathbb{E}[E_t \mid H_0] \le 1 for all t.

Proof. We verify the three defining properties.

(i) Non-negativity. Since X_i \in \{0,1\} and \beta_i \in [0, 1/\theta]:

· If X_i = 1: E_i = 1 + \beta_i(1 - \theta) \ge 1 > 0.
· If X_i = 0: E_i = 1 - \beta_i \theta \ge 1 - (1/\theta) \cdot \theta = 0.

Therefore E_i \ge 0, and the product E_t \ge 0.

(ii) Conditional expectation. Under H_0, \mathbb{E}[X_i \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] = P_{\text{safe}} \ge \theta. Therefore:

\mathbb{E}[E_i \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] = 1 + \beta_i (\mathbb{E}[X_i \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] - \theta) \le 1 + \beta_i (\theta - \theta) = 1,

where the inequality uses \beta_i \ge 0 and \mathbb{E}[X_i \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] \ge \theta. Since \beta_i is \mathcal{F}_{i-1}-measurable (predictable), it can be pulled out of the conditional expectation.

(iii) Supermartingale property. Combining (i) and (ii):

\mathbb{E}[E_t \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] = E_{t-1} \cdot \mathbb{E}[E_t / E_{t-1} \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] = E_{t-1} \cdot \mathbb{E}[E_t \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] \le E_{t-1}.

Therefore E_t is a supermartingale. Taking expectations:

\mathbb{E}[E_t] = \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}[E_t \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}]] \le \mathbb{E}[E_{t-1}] \le \cdots \le \mathbb{E}[E_0] = 1. \qquad \square

A.9 Proof of Theorem 2.6 (Ville's Inequality)

Theorem 2.6. Under H_0, \Pr(\exists t: E_t \ge 1/\alpha \mid H_0) \le \alpha.

Proof. Let E_t be a non-negative supermartingale with \mathbb{E}[E_0] \le 1. Define the stopping time:

\tau = \inf\{t \ge 0 : E_t \ge 1/\alpha\}.

By the optional stopping theorem for non-negative supermartingales:

\mathbb{E}[E_{\tau \wedge T}] \le \mathbb{E}[E_0] \le 1

for any finite T. On the event \{\tau \le T\}, we have E_{\tau \wedge T} = E_\tau \ge 1/\alpha. Therefore:

\frac{1}{\alpha} \Pr(\tau \le T) \le \mathbb{E}[E_{\tau \wedge T} \cdot \mathbb{1}[\tau \le T]] \le \mathbb{E}[E_{\tau \wedge T}] \le 1.

Thus \Pr(\tau \le T) \le \alpha. Taking T \to \infty:

\Pr(\exists t: E_t \ge 1/\alpha) = \Pr(\tau < \infty) \le \alpha. \qquad \square

A.10 Proof of Theorem 2.7 (Regularized Anytime-Validity)

Theorem 2.7. Under H_0, the regularized e-process satisfies:

\Pr(\exists t: E_t^{\text{reg}} \ge 1/\alpha \mid H_0) \le \alpha

for any \alpha \in [0,1], any N, and any data-dependent stopping rule.

Proof. The regularized e-value is defined as:

E_i^{\text{reg}} = \frac{\rho(\Theta) \cdot 1_{\rho(\Theta) < 1} + E_i \cdot \rho(\Theta) \cdot 1_{\rho(\Theta) \ge 1}}{\rho(\Theta)}.

We verify that E_t^{\text{reg}} is a non-negative supermartingale under H_0. The regularization \rho(\Theta) is a knowledge-based, imprecise-probabilistic prior chosen before data collection, hence \mathcal{F}_{0}-measurable. The betting fraction \beta_i remains \mathcal{F}_{i-1}-measurable (predictable).

Consider the conditional expectation:

\mathbb{E}[E_i^{\text{reg}} \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] = \frac{\rho(\Theta) \cdot 1_{\rho(\Theta) < 1} + \mathbb{E}[E_i \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] \cdot \rho(\Theta) \cdot 1_{\rho(\Theta) \ge 1}}{\rho(\Theta)}.

Under H_0, \mathbb{E}[E_i \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] \le 1. Therefore:

\mathbb{E}[E_i^{\text{reg}} \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] \le \frac{\rho(\Theta) \cdot 1_{\rho(\Theta) < 1} + 1 \cdot \rho(\Theta) \cdot 1_{\rho(\Theta) \ge 1}}{\rho(\Theta)} = \frac{\rho(\Theta)}{\rho(\Theta)} = 1.

Non-negativity follows from E_i \ge 0, \rho(\Theta) > 0, and the indicator functions. Therefore E_t^{\text{reg}} is a non-negative supermartingale under H_0.

The generalized Ville's inequality for regularized e-processes establishes that inference based on E_t^{\text{reg}} is valid for any data-dependent stopping rule. Specifically, for any stopping time \tau:

\Pr(\exists t: E_t^{\text{reg}} \ge 1/\alpha) \le \alpha.

The knowledge-dependent regularization does not violate the supermartingale property because \rho(\Theta) is fixed before data collection. \square

