Appendix B: Proofs for Section 3 (Capability Lattice)

B.1 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Faceted Capability Lattice)

Definition B.1 (Prefix-Tree Resource Lattice). Let U be a countable universe of resource identifiers. A prefix pattern is a string p such that p* denotes the set \{u \in U : p \text{ is a prefix of } u\}. Let \mathcal{P} be the set of all prefix patterns, and let \mathcal{R} be the set of all finite unions of prefix patterns, ordered by inclusion. Then (\mathcal{R}, \subseteq) is a complete lattice with:

· Join: R_1 \vee R_2 = R_1 \cup R_2.
· Meet: R_1 \wedge R_2 = R_1 \cap R_2.
· Top: U.
· Bottom: \varnothing.

Proof. The powerset lattice (2^U, \subseteq) is a complete lattice. The set \mathcal{R} is closed under finite unions and intersections of prefix patterns. The completion of \mathcal{R} under arbitrary unions and intersections is the full powerset lattice 2^U, which is complete. For practical purposes, we restrict to finite unions, which suffice for capability representation. \square

Theorem 3.1 (Faceted Capability Lattice). The set of capabilities (\mathcal{C}, \preceq) forms a complete lattice.

Proof. A capability is a tuple:

C = (R, A, \mathcal{C}_{\text{spend}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{depth}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{time}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{rep}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{val}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{rev}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{comp}}).

Each component has a complete lattice structure:

1. Resource order: (\mathcal{R}, \subseteq) is a complete lattice (Definition B.1).
2. Action order: (2^{\mathcal{A}}, \subseteq) is a complete lattice (powerset lattice).
3. Spend order: ([0, \infty], \le) with reverse order (lower is more restrictive) is a complete lattice. The meet is \max and the join is \min.
4. Depth order: (\mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}, \le) with reverse order is a complete lattice. The meet is \max and the join is \min.
5. Time order: (2^{\mathbb{T}}, \subseteq) is a complete lattice (powerset lattice of time intervals).
6. Reputation order: ([0,1], \ge) with direct order (higher is more restrictive) is a complete lattice. The meet is \min and the join is \max.
7. Values order: (2^{\mathcal{V}}, \subseteq) is a complete lattice (powerset lattice of value constraints).
8. Reversibility order: ([0,1], \ge) with direct order is a complete lattice. The meet is \min and the join is \max.
9. Compensability order: (\{\text{none}, \text{partial}, \text{full}\}, \preceq_{\text{comp}}) with \text{none} \preceq \text{partial} \preceq \text{full} is a complete lattice.

The product of complete lattices is a complete lattice (Birkhoff, 1967, Theorem 2). Therefore (\mathcal{C}, \preceq) is a complete lattice. \square

B.2 Proof of Theorem 3.2 (Privilege Non-Escalation)

Theorem 3.2. Let each delegation edge (T_i \to T_{i+1}) be signed with an EUF-CMA-secure signature scheme \Sigma. Let capability attenuation be monotone: for every parent/child pair, C_{i+1} \preceq C_i. Then communication between agents cannot grant new privileges absent an explicit signed delegation.

Proof. We proceed by contradiction. Suppose agent A_1 sends a message m to A_2, and A_2 attempts to execute an action a with capability C_a such that C_a \not\preceq C_1, where C_1 is the capability of A_1. We show that this execution must fail unless there exists a signed delegation edge from A_1 (or an ancestor) to A_2.

(i) Execution requires a valid chain. By the central invariant (§1.3), execution of a requires a valid delegation chain C' = (C_0 \to C_1 \to \cdots \to C_n) where C_n is the capability of A_2 and C_n \succeq C_a. The chain must be signed at every edge.

(ii) The chain must originate from a trusted root. By the IBCT schema, the chain originates from a root issuer. The root's capability C_0 is the maximum authority in the system.

(iii) Attenuation is monotone. By assumption, for every edge (T_i \to T_{i+1}), we have C_{i+1} \preceq C_i. Therefore, by transitivity of \preceq, C_n \preceq C_0.

(iv) Communication does not create edges. The message m from A_1 to A_2 is not a signed delegation edge. It does not appear in any chain. Therefore, the chain C' must contain a signed edge (T_1 \to T_2) where T_1 is an ancestor of A_1 and T_2 is A_2 (or an ancestor of A_2).

(v) Forgery requires breaking EUF-CMA. If such an edge does not exist, then A_2 must forge it. Forging the signature on the edge reduces to the EUF-CMA security of \Sigma. Since \Sigma is EUF-CMA-secure, the forgery succeeds with negligible probability.

Therefore, communication cannot grant new privileges absent a signed delegation edge. \square

B.3 Proof of Theorem 3.3 (Serialization Determinism)

Theorem 3.3. For any two capabilities C_1, C_2, if C_1 = C_2 as tuples, then \text{ser}(C_1) = \text{ser}(C_2) as byte strings.

Proof. The serialization algorithm is deterministic:

1. Dimensions are sorted lexicographically by their names — deterministic.
2. Resource identifiers are serialized as UTF-8 strings with length prefixes — deterministic.
3. Action sets are sorted lexicographically, deduplicated, and serialized as length-prefixed concatenations — deterministic.
4. Numeric values are encoded as 64-bit big-endian integers — deterministic.
5. Time intervals are sorted by start time, deduplicated, and serialized as 64-bit timestamps — deterministic.
6. Value constraints are sorted lexicographically and serialized as length-prefixed strings — deterministic.
7. Compensability is serialized as a single byte — deterministic.

Therefore, identical capabilities produce identical serializations. \square

B.4 Proof of Theorem 3.4 (Revocation Soundness)

Theorem 3.4. Given a Bloom filter \mathcal{B} of revoked chain IDs, no revoked chain is executed:

\text{CID}(C) \in \mathcal{B} \;\Rightarrow\; \text{Verify}(C) = \text{DENY}.

Proof. Bloom filters have no false negatives for inserted elements: if an element x is inserted into \mathcal{B}, then the membership query \mathcal{B}.\text{contains}(x) returns true (Bloom, 1970). Therefore, if \text{CID}(C) is revoked, it is inserted into \mathcal{B}, and \mathcal{B}.\text{contains}(\text{CID}(C)) returns true. The verification procedure checks \mathcal{B}.\text{contains}(\text{CID}(C)) and returns DENY if true. Therefore, no revoked chain is executed. \square

Remark B.1 (False Positives). Bloom filters have a bounded false-positive rate \varepsilon_{\text{fp}}. A non-revoked chain may be incorrectly denied with probability \varepsilon_{\text{fp}}. This is a liveness cost, not a safety cost.

