Appendix C: Proofs for Section 4 (Architecture)

C.1 Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Two-Phase Safety)

Theorem 4.1. No high-risk action executes without slow-path confirmation or a registered compensating action.

Proof. By Definition 4.1, for actions with risk \ge \tau_{\text{fast}}:

1. Phase 1 (Prepare): Fast-path issues PROVISIONAL_ALLOW and the action is held. A durable audit record is written to the WAL with fsync.
2. Phase 2 (Commit/Abort): Slow-path evaluates. If ALLOW, commit and execute. If DENY, abort.

The action is held in Phase 1 and only released in Phase 2 upon explicit slow-path approval or with a registered compensating action. For non-compensable actions (\text{comp}(a) = \text{none}), Routing Rule 4.1 mandates human approval, which is a strict form of slow-path confirmation. There is no code path from PROVISIONAL_ALLOW to execution that bypasses Phase 2. Therefore, no high-risk action executes without slow-path confirmation or a registered compensating action. \square

C.2 Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Two-Phase Liveness)

Theorem 4.2. Low-risk actions are not indefinitely delayed.

Proof. Low-risk actions (\text{risk} < \tau_{\text{fast}}) bypass the slow path entirely and execute on the fast path. The risk budget (token bucket) ensures that fast-path decisions are rate-limited: each fast-path execution consumes \hat{r}(a) + \epsilon_r tokens, and when the bucket is exhausted, subsequent actions wait for the slow path. The token bucket refills at rate \rho, so the system cannot starve indefinitely. \square

C.3 Proof of Proposition 4.1 (Risk Budget Bound with Error)

Proposition 4.1. The risk budget bounds the expected risk of fast-path executions:

\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t \in \text{fast}} r(a_t)\right] \le \rho \cdot T + B.

Proof. Let N_{\text{fast}}(t) be the number of fast-path executions up to time t. The token bucket enforces:

\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{fast}}(t)} (\hat{r}(a_i) + \epsilon_r) \le \rho t + B

for all t, since the bucket starts with at most B tokens and refills at rate \rho. The true risk satisfies r(a_i) \le \hat{r}(a_i) + \epsilon_r by definition of the estimation error bound. Therefore:

\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{fast}}(t)} r(a_i) \le \sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{fast}}(t)} (\hat{r}(a_i) + \epsilon_r) \le \rho t + B.

Taking expectations and letting t \to T:

\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t \in \text{fast}} r(a_t)\right] \le \rho T + B. \qquad \square

C.4 Saga-Style Compensation Specification

The two-phase admission protocol for high-risk actions employs a saga-style compensation pattern. Each step in a multi-step action sequence is associated with a forward action and an optional compensating action. If any step fails or is denied by the slow path, all previously completed steps are compensated in reverse order (LIFO).

Formally, let a multi-step action be \pi = (a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n) with compensating actions (\gamma_1, \gamma_2, \ldots, \gamma_n). If step k fails, the compensation executes \gamma_{k-1}, \gamma_{k-2}, \ldots, \gamma_1 in that order. The system is guaranteed to reach a state equivalent to the initial state if all compensations are idempotent and total.

Proposition C.1 (Saga Consistency). If all compensating actions are idempotent and total, and the forward actions are executed sequentially, then the saga either completes all forward actions or returns the system to the initial state.

Proof. By induction on the number of steps. If n = 1, the saga either commits a_1 or aborts and executes \gamma_1, which restores the initial state by totality. For n > 1, if step k fails, the compensation \gamma_{k-1}, \ldots, \gamma_1 restores the initial state by the induction hypothesis. Idempotence ensures that repeated compensation does not alter the restored state. \square

