Appendix D: Proofs for Section 5 (Technical Specifications)

D.1 Proof of Theorem 5.1 (Soundness and Completeness of Compilation)

Theorem 5.1. The compilation function is sound and complete for all templates in the Goal Envelope AST.

Proof. By structural induction on the template grammar.

Base cases:

BOUNDED_ACTION_SEQUENCE. The denotation is:

\llbracket \text{BOUNDED\_ACTION\_SEQUENCE}(A, M, F) \rrbracket = \{\tau \mid \forall t: \tau_t \in A,\ \forall a: \#_a(\tau) \le M[a],\ \forall (f,t) \in F: \neg(f \prec t \text{ in } \tau)\}.

The compilation is:

\text{compile} = G\left( \bigwedge_{a \in A} X a \right) \wedge G\left( \bigwedge_{(f,t) \in F} (f \to X \neg t) \right) \wedge \bigwedge_{a \in M} (\#a \le M[a]).

A trace satisfies the first conjunct iff every action is in A. It satisfies the second conjunct iff no forbidden transition occurs. It satisfies the third conjunct iff the occurrence count of each action is bounded. Therefore soundness and completeness hold.

DENY_TRANSITION. The denotation is \{\tau \mid \forall t': \tau_{t'} = f \Rightarrow \tau_{t'+1} \neq t\}. The compilation is G(f \to X \neg t). Soundness and completeness follow directly.

REQUIRE_PRIOR. The denotation is \{\tau \mid \forall t': \tau_{t'} = t \Rightarrow \exists t'' < t': \tau_{t''} = p\}. The compilation is G(t \to F p). Soundness and completeness follow from the semantics of the F operator.

RATE_LIMIT. The denotation is \{\tau \mid \forall t': \#_a(\tau_{t' \ldots t'+w}) \le m\}. The compilation is G(\#a \le m \ U \ (w \text{ elapsed})). Soundness and completeness follow from the semantics of the U operator.

Compositional cases:

AND. \llbracket \text{AND}(T_1, T_2) \rrbracket = \llbracket T_1 \rrbracket \cap \llbracket T_2 \rrbracket. The compilation is \text{compile}(T_1) \wedge \text{compile}(T_2). By the induction hypothesis, \tau \models \text{compile}(T_i) \iff \tau \in \llbracket T_i \rrbracket. Therefore \tau \models \text{compile}(T_1) \wedge \text{compile}(T_2) \iff \tau \in \llbracket T_1 \rrbracket \cap \llbracket T_2 \rrbracket.

OR. \llbracket \text{OR}(T_1, T_2) \rrbracket = \llbracket T_1 \rrbracket \cup \llbracket T_2 \rrbracket. The compilation is \text{compile}(T_1) \vee \text{compile}(T_2). By the induction hypothesis, soundness and completeness follow.

UNTIL. \llbracket \text{UNTIL}(T_1, T_2) \rrbracket = \{\tau \mid \exists t: \tau_{t \ldots} \in \llbracket T_2 \rrbracket \wedge \forall t' < t: \tau_{t' \ldots} \in \llbracket T_1 \rrbracket\}. The compilation is \text{compile}(T_1) \ U \ \text{compile}(T_2). By the induction hypothesis, soundness and completeness follow. \square

D.2 Proof of Theorem 5.2 (Trace-Checking Complexity for LTL_f)

Theorem 5.2. For any LTL_f safety or cosafety formula \varphi, monitoring can be carried out by direct trace-checking with polynomial complexity in |\varphi| and |\tau|, without constructing a DFA.

Proof. The result follows from Brunello et al. (2026), who show that all formulas in the safety and cosafety fragments of LTL_f are intentionally safe and intentionally cosafe. For such formulas, the trace-checking problem reduces to evaluating the formula on the finite trace using dynamic programming over the formula structure. The dynamic program has:

· State space: O(|\varphi| \cdot |\tau|) subproblems (one per subformula and trace position).
· Transition: O(1) per subproblem (standard Boolean and temporal operators).
· Total complexity: O(|\varphi| \cdot |\tau|).

This is polynomial in both the formula size and the trace length, and avoids the doubly exponential blow-up of DFA construction. \square

D.3 Proof of Hardened Seccomp Non-Bypassability

Definition D.1 (Hardened Seccomp Filter). A seccomp filter F is hardened if it satisfies:

1. ABI validation: F checks AUDIT_ARCH and masks off the x32 bit before comparing syscall numbers.
2. Full argument check: F reads both the low and high 32 bits of syscall arguments.
3. FD restriction: F returns SECCOMP_RET_TRACE for dup, dup2, dup3, and fcntl.
4. Network allowlist: A cgroup BPF egress firewall at connect4/connect6 hooks enforces a per-session allowlist.

Theorem D.1 (Non-Bypassability). Let F be a hardened seccomp filter with syscall surface \Sigma_{\text{allowed}} \subseteq \Sigma_{\text{all}}. Then:

\text{Execute}(q) \;\Longrightarrow\; F(q) \wedge \text{landlock}(q) \wedge \text{netfilter}(q) \wedge \text{ACP-admitted}(q).

Proof. Any q requiring \sigma \notin \Sigma_{\text{allowed}} is blocked at the kernel boundary with EPERM. The ABI validation prevents x32 aliasing, the full argument check prevents 32-bit truncation bypass, the FD restriction prevents file descriptor remapping, and the network allowlist prevents unauthorized egress. Therefore, no q can bypass the filter. The Landlock LSM provides filesystem confinement, and the netfilter/cgroup BPF provides network confinement. The ACP admission is enforced at the application layer. \square

