Appendix L: Numerical Estimates for Component-Wise PAC Bounds

L.1 Uniform Bound (Baseline)

For a deployment with N = 5000 abstract states, H = 100, L_\psi = 2.0, \varepsilon_{\text{abs}} = 0.01, \delta = 0.05, and desired \varepsilon = 0.1, with observed variance \hat{\sigma}^2 = 0.01:

n \ge \frac{2 \cdot 0.01 \cdot \log(10^9)}{(0.1 - 0.02)^2 / (2 \cdot 100)^2} + \frac{7 \log(10^9)}{3 \cdot ((0.1 - 0.02) / (2 \cdot 100))^2} \approx 2.6 \times 10^6 + 3.0 \times 10^8.

This is prohibitive for uniform bounds.

L.2 Component-Wise Bound (Proposed)

With sensitivity-weighted allocation, suppose L_\psi^{(ij)} \propto 1/(i+j) (power-law decay). The effective number of sensitive transitions is:

N_{\text{eff}} = \sum_{i,j} \frac{L_\psi^{(ij)}}{L_\psi^{\max}} \approx N \log N \approx 5000 \cdot 8.5 \approx 4.25 \times 10^4.

The sample complexity reduces to:

n_{ij} \ge \frac{7 L_\psi^{(ij)} H \log(2/\delta_{ij})}{3 \varepsilon_{ij}} \approx \frac{7 \cdot 0.5 \cdot 100 \cdot \log(2 \cdot 10^5)}{3 \cdot 0.02} \approx 2.4 \times 10^4.

This is a reduction of four orders of magnitude compared to the uniform bound.

L.3 Sequential Bound (Fixed-Confidence)

Using a fixed-confidence sequential bound with the e-process, the expected sample size to achieve \varepsilon = 0.1 with confidence 1 - \delta = 0.95 is:

\mathbb{E}[n] \approx \frac{2 \log(1/\alpha)}{\mathrm{KL}(P_{\text{alt}} \| P_{\text{null}})} \approx \frac{2 \log(20)}{0.01} \approx 600.

This is a further reduction of two orders of magnitude, at the cost of requiring a fixed alternative for the SPRT.

